  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 215 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nFriday Night through Wednesday:\n\nA large cutoff 500mb trough will continue eastward toward the \nAtlantic Coast Friday night into Saturday. Canadian high pressure \nwill settle into the upper Great Lakes and western Corn Belt Region \nFriday night. A colder air mass, radiational cooling and light winds \nwill allow overnight lows to drop into the low to mid 20s away from \nLake Michigan.\n\nSaturday, 850mb winds will continue to come around to northerly and \nnorthwesterly through the day into the nighttime hours. This will \npush a lingering lake effect band over Lake Michigan away from the \nWisconsin shoreline, keeping us dry. High pressure should support \nlight north winds and mostly sunny skies.\n\nHigh pressure is then expected to linger through the extended period \nkeeping the weather sunny, dry, and quiet. There will be a muted \nwarming trend, with highs around normal through Monday, and then in \nthe low to mid 50s by Tuesday and Wednesday as some modest upper \nlevel ridging tries to develop over the central plains.\n\nCMiller",
